Now add tests for both. Update `sub-issue-report.test.ts`:

Add tests:
1. dispatch-marker line: 
```ts
test("a dispatch marker in the child's report is not the machinery asking for an agent", () => {
  const body = subIssueReportBody({
    child: 12,
    report: "The work is delivered.\n\n<!-- atomaton:dispatch=engineer -->",
    pullRequests: [],
  });
  expect(parseCommentCommand(proseOnly(body)).agent).toBe("");
  expect(DISPATCH_TAG.hasProse(body)).toBe(false);
  expect(body).toContain("The work is delivered.");
});
```
Hmm — after `withoutTags`, the dispatch line is stripped entirely, so `DISPATCH_TAG.hasProse(body)` false and no ask. That's the first defence. But I want to also pin the second defence (defuseCommandStart) for a marker that survives stripping — e.g. a malformed spelling that `DISPATCH_RE` accepts but `withoutTags` does not strip. DISPATCH_RE accepts `<!--atomaton:dispatch = engineer-->`? Let's see: `^<!--\s*atomaton:dispatch\s*=\s*(AGENT_NAME_PATTERN)\s*-->`. The tag writer pattern in tags.ts is `<!--\\s*atomaton:dispatch=(?:pattern)\\s*-->` (from makeTag, no `\s*` before `=`). So `<!-- atomaton:dispatch =engineer -->` would match DISPATCH_RE (a command to the thread reader) but NOT the registered tag pattern (no whitespace tolerance around `=`), so `withoutTags` leaves it → then `defuseCommandStart` escapes `<` → no longer a command. 

That's a great test of the second defence: a hand-retyped marker. The comment in comment-command.ts even says "The whitespace tolerance around `=` is likewise wider than the tag writer ever emits -- it costs nothing and this is the one place reading a marker a human may have retyped by hand."

So test:
```ts
test("a dispatch marker the tag stripper does not recognise is still not a request", () => {
  // `DISPATCH_RE` tolerates whitespace the tag writer never emits, so a hand-retyped
  // marker is a command to the thread reader and invisible to `withoutTags`. This is
  // the case the escape has to cover on its own.
  const body = subIssueReportBody({
    child: 12,
    report: "See below.\n\n<!-- atomaton:dispatch = engineer -->",
    pullRequests: [],
  });
  expect(parseCommentCommand(proseOnly(body)).agent).toBe("");
  expect(body).toContain("\\<!-- atomaton:dispatch = engineer -->");
});
```
Wait — will the escaped line render? `\<!--` in markdown → renders `<!--` visible? CommonMark: an escaped `<` becomes literal `<`, displayed as text `<!-- atomaton:dispatch = engineer -->`. Good.

Careful: the body would then contain `<details>` etc. and `parseCommentCommand(proseOnly(body))` — does any other line parse? No.

Hmm, but hold on: does `body` still contain `<!--` at all? After escaping, `\<!--` — `proseOnly` doesn't care. `parseCommentCommand` line trim → `\<!-- atomaton:dispatch = engineer -->` doesn't start with `/` and DISPATCH_RE requires `^<!--` → no match ✓.

2. Also a plain dispatch marker (exact writer form) → stripped by withoutTags:
```ts
test("a tag-shaped marker in the child's report is not read as parent state", () => {
  const body = subIssueReportBody({
    child: 12,
    report: "Delivered.\n\n<!-- atomaton:aggregated=12 -->\n\n<!-- atomaton:dispatch=engineer -->",
    pullRequests: [],
  });
  expect(AGGREGATED_TAG.readProse(body)).toBeUndefined();
  expect(DISPATCH_TAG.hasProse(body)).toBe(false);
  // the composed body's own context tag survives
  expect(body).toContain("<!-- atomaton:llm-context=include -->");
  expect(body).toContain("Delivered.");
});
```

Note `AGGREGATED_TAG.readProse(body)` — would `<!-- atomaton:llm-context=include -->` interfere? No.

Careful: the existing first test asserts `AGGREGATED_TAG.readProse(body)` undefined; keep.

Also need imports: `DISPATCH_TAG`.

Let me write these two tests.

